course: dictionary
lesson: 133
title: Spotting the advert
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An advert is a short message, picture or film that tells people about something to buy or do, to make them want it. It is short for advertisement, and you can also write it as ad. An advert can also tell people about a job, an event or a lost pet.
explain: Adverts try to persuade you, which means they try to make you want something. «"Clinically proven" in an advert» can sound very strong, but it may mean only a tiny test. Ask who tested it, on how many people, and compared with what.
explain: Adverts are easy to spot on TV, buses and websites, but paid ones are sometimes marked «ad» or «sponsored», and that label can be in small print. Look for it before you trust the message.
fact: Advert is short for advertisement, and ad means the same thing.
fact: An advert can sell a thing, or tell people about a job, an event or a lost pet.
fact: «Videos that are paid adverts are often marked "ad" or "sponsored", sometimes in small print.»
fact: A picture on a box is an advert, while the ingredients list tells the truth.
fact: «Food adverts aimed at children have rules about what they can show.»
exercise: «Videos that are paid adverts are often marked "ad" or "sponsored", sometimes in small print.» What does ad mean? | A type of cheese | A long film | A kind of video game | *A short way of saying advert
exercise: Which of these can an advert tell you about? | The rules of chess | The time of the next bus | *A lost cat | The speed of light
exercise: «"Clinically proven" in an advert can mean a tiny test without fair comparison.» What does this suggest? | The product is free | *The test may be small and not fair | The test was done on thousands of people in every country | The advert is always true
test: «Watch for shrinkflation, when a bar shrinks from 200 g to 180 g at the same £2.» What is shrinkflation? | A bar is given away for free | A bar gets bigger and costs less | A bar is made with no sugar | *A bar gets smaller but still costs the same
test: Why should you read the ingredients list on a box? | It is always written in big letters | It has the funniest picture on it | It is the advert's trick | *It tells the truth about what is in the food
exam: Maya says, «The advert shows a picture on the box, so the box must be telling the truth.» What is the best answer? | Yes, pictures never lie | *No, a picture on a box is an advert, so check the ingredients list | Yes, because the advert says so | No, boxes never have pictures
